{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.no_initial_gap","record":{"id":"support.no_initial_gap","kind":"claim","label":"No initial gap when zero is outside the support","status":"unverified","source_id":"support_tex_variation","locator":"variation.tex:239–242; initial-gap case","statement":"For an admissible minimizer gamma, an initial support-complement component [0,b), with 0<b<1 and b in S, is impossible. Since mu([0,b))=0, gamma=0 on [0,b). The zero-interval result gives q'(t)<q'(b)<=1 for 0<t<b, but symmetry gives q(0)=0 and contact at b gives q(b)=b. Integration contradicts these two values.","assumption_ids":["scalar.paper_minimizer"],"evidence_ids":["support_source_bindings_20261024","support_graph_semantic_review_pending"],"citation_ids":["support_closure_source","support_closure_lean_source","support_contact_source"],"boundary":"Curated source-level mathematical statement, not an elaborated Lean extraction or independently accepted proof. Its displayed local hypotheses are quantified guards; a hypothetical plateau is not an added premise of the full-support theorem. Zero is not assumed to be a support point here. No left endpoint slope/contact condition is imported from the bounded-gap case; only q(0)=0 is used. The minimizer premise here belongs to the ordinary manuscript model; formal-interface hypotheses remain on the separate Lean target. This classification does not prove a transport between formal models."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.no_initial_gap","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["mapped_claim"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaps","anchor":"gaps","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:7b07c474e9e4b493474081a793473deb1d5ecae2fdcc29bf9c00dc4caa8bbe8a/papers/short.md/sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/passages/gap-proof","anchor":"gap-proof","mapping_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/passages/endpoints","anchor":"endpoints","mapping_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/gaps","anchor":"gaps","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n**Theorem 5.1 (full support).** In the pure zero-field SK model with\n$\\xi(t)=t^2/2$, let $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$ minimize $\\mathcal P$. Then\n$\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_\\gamma=[0,1)$ and $\\gamma$ is strictly increasing.\nFor every associated diffusion started at zero,\n$\\mathbb E u(t,X_t)^2=t$ and $\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$ for\n$0\\le t<1$.\n\nKeep the same minimiser, diffusion and moment function $q$ throughout.\nSection 3 has established $G\\ge0$, $G=0$ on the support\n$S=\\operatorname{supp}\\mu_\\gamma$, and the contact conditions\n$q(s)=s$, $q'(s)\\le1$ at support points. At zero, the value identity\ncomes from symmetry, and the slope bound requires $0\\in S$.\nSection 4 supplies curvature on constant intervals for every admissible\ncoefficient. These premises are available before any support gap is\nexcluded. We first deal with the endpoint at one, then use the curvature\nestimate on the remaining intervals.\n\nFor a terminal interval $(a,1)$, including empty support $[0,1)$,\nthe cusp estimate gives $1-q(t)\\gtrsim\\sqrt{1-t}$ and hence $G<0$\nnear one. On a positive plateau with two contact endpoints, strict\nconvexity puts $q'$ below its endpoint chord, although its integral\nmust equal the interval length. On a zero plateau the heat argument\nmakes $q'$ strictly increasing, below the right contact slope.\nAt an initial gap, use the one-sided contact slope if $0\\in S$;\notherwise the coefficient is zero and symmetry supplies $q(0)=0$.\n\n<a id=\"terminal-gap\"></a>\n\n**Why a terminal plateau violates the variational inequality.** Suppose that $\\gamma=c$ on $(a,1)$, where $0\\le a<1$ and\n$c\\ge0$ is finite. The endpoint $1$ is outside the support domain, so\nthere is no contact condition there to complete an endpoint-chord\nargument. Instead we will prove\n$$\n1-q(t)\\ge C\\sqrt{1-t}\n\\tag{5.1}\n$$\nfor some $C>0$ and every $t$ sufficiently close to one. The proof\nseparates two facts: the diffusion puts positive probability density\nnear zero, and the smoothed terminal slope has a definite deficit from\n$\\pm1$ in a shrinking window around zero.\n\nFix $s\\in(a,1)$ and let $\\rho_s$ be the law of $X_s$. Choose\n$R<\\infty$ such that $p_0=\\rho_s([-R,R])>0$. For $c>0$, set\n$$\nh(t,y)=\\mathbb E e^{c|y+\\sqrt{1-t}Z|},\\qquad\n\\Phi(t,y)=c^{-1}\\log h(t,y),\n$$\nwhere $Z$ is standard normal. The drift on this interval is\n$c\\Phi_x=h_x/h$, and the transition density is\n$$\nk_{s,t}(x,y)=p_{t-s}(y-x)\\frac{h(t,y)}{h(s,x)},\\qquad\np_v(z)=(2\\pi v)^{-1/2}e^{-z^2/(2v)}.\n\\tag{5.2}\n$$\nThe heat-semigroup identity normalises this kernel; equivalently, for\nBrownian motion started at $(s,x)$, $h(t,B_t)/h(s,x)$ is the positive\nmean-one change of measure which adds the drift $h_x/h$. For $c=0$,\nthe same kernel formula applies\nwith $h=1$, and the value function is the ordinary heat evolution of\n$|x|$. [OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3, proof of (6.6)][fs-var].\n\nPut $L=1-s$ and $\\delta=L/2$. For\n$s+\\delta\\le t<1$, $|x|\\le R$ and $|y|\\le1$,\n$$\np_{t-s}(y-x)\\ge\n(2\\pi L)^{-1/2}e^{-(R+1)^2/(2\\delta)}=:k_*>0,\n$$\nwhile $h(t,y)\\ge1$ and\n$h(s,x)\\le e^{cR}\\mathbb E e^{c\\sqrt L|Z|}=:M<\\infty$.\nIntegrating (5.2) over the part of $\\rho_s$ in $[-R,R]$ therefore\ngives a density $f_t$ of $X_t$ with\n$$\nf_t(y)\\ge p_0k_*/M=:m>0\n\\quad(s+\\delta\\le t<1,\\ |y|\\le1).\n\\tag{5.3}\n$$\nOnly positive mass of a compact set was needed at time $s$; no density\nassumption on $\\rho_s$ has entered.\n\nNow write $\\varepsilon=1-t$ and restrict to\n$|y|\\le\\sqrt\\varepsilon$. Differentiating the terminal heat formula\ngives, with the unweighted interpretation for $c=0$,\n$$\nu(t,y)=\n\\frac{\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{sgn}(y+\\sqrt\\varepsilon Z)\n                     e^{c|y+\\sqrt\\varepsilon Z|}]}\n     {\\mathbb E e^{c|y+\\sqrt\\varepsilon Z|}}.\n$$\nThe denominator is at most\n$D=e^c\\mathbb E e^{c|Z|}<\\infty$. Each sign has unnormalised\nmass at least $p_*:=\\mathbb P(Z\\ge1)>0$: the threshold\n$|y/\\sqrt\\varepsilon|$ is at most one, and the exponential weight\nis at least one. Hence each sign has tilted probability at least\n$\\theta=p_*/D\\in(0,1/2)$. It follows that\n$$\n|u(t,y)|\\le1-2\\theta,\\qquad\n1-u(t,y)^2\\ge4\\theta(1-\\theta)=:\\kappa>0.\n$$\nMultiplying this uniform deficit by the density lower bound (5.3)\nand the window width $2\\sqrt\\varepsilon$ yields\n$$\n1-q(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1-u(t,y)^2)f_t(y)\\,dy\n\\ge 2m\\kappa\\sqrt{1-t}.\n$$\nThis proves (5.1) with $C=2m\\kappa$.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3, density and terminal-window estimates][fs-var].\n\nThe sign of the contradiction is now explicit. For $s'$ sufficiently\nclose to one, the estimate holds at every $t\\in[s',1)$, so\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nG(s')&=\\int_{s'}^1(q(t)-t)\\,dt\\\\\n&\\le\\frac{(1-s')^2}{2}\n       -\\frac{2C}{3}(1-s')^{3/2}<0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe final inequality holds once $\\sqrt{1-s'}<4C/3$. It contradicts\nthe nonnegative cost of adding an atom at $s'$. This proof covers\n$c=0$ as well as $c>0$. In particular, empty support is impossible:\nthen $\\mu_\\gamma=0$ and $\\gamma=0$ on all of $[0,1)$.\nThe terminal-plateau conclusion was already recorded in\n[Auffinger, Chen and Zeng (2020), Remark 7][acz]; the estimates above\nsupply the quantitative argument needed here.\n\n<a id=\"qualitative-terminal-gap\"></a>\n\n**A shorter qualitative exclusion.** The existing curvature input also\nexcludes a terminal support gap without estimating its boundary layer.\nHere is the elementary calculus step. Suppose\n$q\\in C^1([r,1))$, $q(r)=r$, $q'(r)\\le1$, $q\\le1$, and $q'$\nis strictly convex on $(r,1)$. If $q(t)\\ge t$ at some $r<t<1$,\nstrict integration below the finite endpoint chord gives\n$$\nt-r\\le\\int_r^t q'(v)\\,dv\n <\\frac{t-r}{2}\\bigl(q'(r)+q'(t)\\bigr),\n$$\nso $q'(t)>1$. For $t<s<1$, convexity then gives\n$$\nq'(s)\\ge q'(t)+\\frac{s-t}{t-r}\\bigl(q'(t)-q'(r)\\bigr)\n\\ge q'(t)>1.\n$$\nConsequently $q(s)\\ge t+(s-t)q'(t)>1$ for $s$ sufficiently\nclose to one, a contradiction. Thus $q(t)<t$ throughout $(r,1)$.\nAll chord endpoints lie below one; no derivative at one is used.\n\nTo apply this argument independently of the quantitative proof, first\nexclude empty support by the [zero-coefficient descent](#zero-coefficient-example):\n$D_+\\mathcal P(0)[1]=1/4-1/\\pi<0$. A nonempty support with a\nterminal complementary component has a last point $r<1$. Its plateau\nhas positive height, since a neighbourhood of $r$ has positive measure.\nThe contact values and slope at $r$, including the one-sided case $r=0$,\nand positive-plateau curvature meet the calculus hypotheses. Since\n$0\\le q\\le1$, integration gives $G(r)<0$, contradicting atom addition.\nThis is an ordinary consequence of the previously stated inputs. The\nquantitative proof above is retained for its stronger estimate (5.1).\n\n**The remaining gaps have a finite right contact point.**\n\nThe support is relatively closed in $[0,1)$. Its complement is a union\nof relative open intervals, and the Stieltjes measure vanishes on each\nsuch interval. For $x<y$ in one interval,\n$$\n\\gamma(y)-\\gamma(x)=\\mu_\\gamma((x,y])=0,\n$$\nso $\\gamma$ is constant there. The terminal argument excludes a component\nending at one. Every remaining component therefore ends at a point\n$b<1$ of $S$.\n\n<a id=\"bounded-gaps\"></a>\n\nFirst consider a gap $(a,b)$ with both endpoints in $S$, allowing\n$a=0$. Compact-strip regularity gives continuity of $q'$ on $[a,b]$,\neven if $\\gamma$ jumps at an endpoint. Contact gives\n$$\nq(a)=a,\\qquad q(b)=b,\\qquad q'(a),q'(b)\\le1.\n\\tag{5.4}\n$$\nIf the constant coefficient is $c>0$, Section 4 makes $q'$ strictly\nconvex in the gap. Continuity extends the chord comparison to its\nendpoints, giving\n$$\nq'(t)<\\frac{b-t}{b-a}q'(a)+\\frac{t-a}{b-a}q'(b)\\le1,\n\\qquad a<t<b.\n\\tag{5.5}\n$$\nIntegrating contradicts $q(b)-q(a)=b-a$. Equivalently, the mean value\ntheorem applied to (5.4) requires an interior point with $q'=1$,\nwhereas (5.5) excludes every such point. The endpoint derivatives need\nonly be at most one; equality has not been assumed. With $h=q'$ and\n$C_{a,b}$ its endpoint chord, the contradiction is the exact area identity\n$$\n(b-a)-\\int_a^b h\n=(b-a)\\left(1-\\frac{h(a)+h(b)}2\\right)\n +\\int_a^b(C_{a,b}-h)>0.\n$$\nThe first term is nonnegative; strict convexity makes the second positive.\nOnly continuity of $q'$ is needed at the ends. For example, a jump\n$\\Delta\\gamma$ at $s$ gives the one-sided identity\n$$\nq''(s+)-q''(s-)=-2\\Delta\\gamma\\,\\mathbb E a(s,X_s)^3.\n$$\nIt follows by applying Itô's formula to $a^2$ and using continuity of\nits derivative moments on a compact strip. Taking $\\gamma=0$ before\n$s=1/2$ and $\\gamma=1/2$ afterward makes this jump strictly negative;\nthe terminal Cole–Hopf solution has positive Hessian. Thus continuity\nof $q'$ does not license classical higher time derivatives across\ncoefficient jumps. [OpenAI (2026a), proof of Corollary 6.4][fs-density].\n\nIf $c=0$, the separate heat argument in Section 4 makes $q'$ strictly\nincreasing. Then $q'(t)<q'(b)\\le1$ for $a<t<b$, and the same\ncontradiction follows. The left slope bound is unnecessary in this case.\n[OpenAI (2026a), proof of Theorem 1.1 after Lemma 6.3][fs-var].\n\n<a id=\"initial-gaps\"></a>\n\nThere are two distinct possibilities at the left endpoint. If\n$(0,b)$ is a gap and $0\\in S$, the preceding proof applies using\nthe one-sided contact slope bound at zero. Because $(0,b)$ contains\nno mass, the support condition at zero forces an atom there and hence\n$c=\\gamma(0)>0$. Its absence has not been proved at this stage.\nIf instead the complementary component is $[0,b)$ with $0\\notin S$,\nthere is no mass anywhere in $[0,b)$. Thus $\\gamma=0$ there.\nThe identity $q(0)=0$ comes from zero-field symmetry, and the\nright endpoint gives $q(b)=b$, $q'(b)\\le1$. Here $q''(t)>0$ for\n$0<t<b$; at zero evenness gives $\\Phi_{xxx}(0,0)=0$ and hence\n$q''(0)=0$. Strict increase on the open interval still forces $q'<1$\nthroughout $(0,b)$, an impossibility.\n\n**Full support and the moment identities.**\n\nThe cases are exhaustive, so $S=[0,1)$. Only now can contact be\napplied at every $0<t<1$, giving $q(t)=t$ throughout the domain;\nthe value at zero was already known. Differentiating and using the\ngradient-martingale identity gives\n$$\n\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=q'(t)=1,\\qquad 0\\le t<1,\n$$\nwith the right derivative at zero. Finally, every nonempty open\nsubinterval $(a,b)$ receives positive mass. Therefore\n$$\n\\gamma(b)-\\gamma(a)=\\mu_\\gamma((a,b])>0\n\\qquad(0\\le a<b<1).\n$$\nThe proof establishes full support, strict increase and the two\nmoment identities. Smooth absolute continuity, including the later\nconclusion $\\gamma(0)=0$, uses the source's further argument from\nsaturated consistency; it has not been assumed to obtain contact\nor to exclude an initial gap. [OpenAI (2026a), conclusion of the\nproof of Theorem 1.1][fs-var].\nA weaker analytic condition suffices for the bounded-gap contradiction.\nIf $h\\in C([a,b])\\cap C^2((a,b))$ has the displayed endpoint and\nintegral data, then $h'=0\\Rightarrow h''>0$ is already impossible:\neither $h\\le1$ everywhere, forcing $h\\equiv1$ by its integral, or it\nattains an interior maximum above one, where $h'=0$ and $h''\\le0$.\nThis is an elementary deduction from these premises. Chen's conditional\ncrossing theorem, with its own hypotheses, is of this form; §4 proves\nthe stronger $q'''>0$ throughout a positive plateau, without a minimizer\npremise. See [Chen, arXiv:2607.18032v1, Proposition 4.2 and the proof\nof Proposition 4.3][chen26]. Neither statement supplies terminal contact\nat the excluded endpoint one.\n","span":{"start_byte":66089,"end_byte":77343,"start_line":1460,"end_line":1720},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaps","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=gaps"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:7b07c474e9e4b493474081a793473deb1d5ecae2fdcc29bf9c00dc4caa8bbe8a/papers/short.md/sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/passages/gap-proof","anchor":"gap-proof","mapping_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n**Lemma 2 (constant-interval curvature; [OpenAI (2026a),\nProposition 5.1][FS]).** Let $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$, with its\nassociated diffusion and moment $q$. If $\\gamma=c$ on an open\ninterval $I\\subset(0,1)$, called a plateau, then for $t\\in I$\n$$\nq'''(t)\\geq\\frac{c^2}{1000}\\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)^4]>0\n\\quad\\text{if }c>0.                               \\tag{3}\n$$\nIf $c=0$, then $q''(t)=\\mathbb E[\\Phi_{xxx}(t,X_t)^2]>0$.\n\nMinimality is not a hypothesis of this lemma. Section 3 derives its\nmoment polynomial and explains the integrated estimate imported from\nthe source.\n\n**Proof of Theorem 1, assuming Lemma 2.** A complementary\ninterval with two support endpoints admits two contact conditions;\nan initial interval without support at zero has coefficient zero;\na terminal interval is handled here by a boundary estimate.\n\nSuppose $(\\ell,r)$ is a support gap with endpoints in $S$ and $r<1$.\nThe coefficient is a positive constant there: support at $\\ell$ and\nabsence of mass immediately to its right force\n$\\mu_\\gamma([0,\\ell])>0$. Strict convexity of $q'$ and (2) give\n$$\nq'(t)<\\frac{r-t}{r-\\ell}q'(\\ell)\n       +\\frac{t-\\ell}{r-\\ell}q'(r)\\leq1\n\\qquad(\\ell<t<r).\n$$\nIntegrating contradicts $q(r)-q(\\ell)=r-\\ell$. The geometric\nobstruction is an area deficit under $q'$.\nAn initial gap $[0,r)$ with $0\\notin S$ and $r\\in S$ has no\nmass, hence $\\gamma=0$ there. Its right endpoint gives $q(r)=r$\nand $q'(r)\\leq1$, while zero field gives $q(0)=0$.\nThe zero-plateau identity gives $q''>0$, hence $q'(t)<q'(r)\\leq1$.\nThe same\nintegral contradiction follows without assuming $G(0)=0$. A gap\n$(0,r)$ with $0\\in S$ is already covered by the two-contact case.\n\nA terminal gap cannot use contact at one, which lies outside the\nsupport domain. Its exclusion was recorded by\n[Auffinger, Chen and Zeng (2020), Remark 7][ACZ].\n[Chen (2026), Proposition 4.5, equations (4.27)–(4.38)][Chen] proves\na positive square-root asymptotic for $1-q(t)$ under the terminal-gap\nhypothesis. We use the sufficient lower bound proved directly in\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3][FS]: on a supposed constant interval\nending at one,\n$$\n1-q(t)\\geq C\\sqrt{1-t}\n$$\nfor some $C>0$ and all $t$ sufficiently close to one. Two estimates\nproduce this scale. The heat representation of the terminal value\n$|x|$ gives $1-u(t,x)^2\\geq\\kappa>0$ on\n$|x|\\leq\\sqrt{1-t}$. A transition-kernel comparison from a fixed\nearlier time gives a density $f_t(x)\\geq m>0$ on the fixed window\n$|x|\\leq1$, uniformly as $t\\uparrow1$. Thus\n$$\n1-q(t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}(1-u(t,x)^2)f_t(x)\\,dx\n\\geq2m\\kappa\\sqrt{1-t}.\n$$\nThe time-uniform density estimate, proved in Lemma 6.3, equations\n(6.6)–(6.7), is essential; pointwise positivity of each density would\nnot suffice. For $h=1-t<C^2$, we have $q(t)-t\\leq h-C\\sqrt h<0$,\nso $G(s)<0$ for $s$ sufficiently close to one. This contradicts\nminimality and also excludes empty support. An alternative proof from\nplateau curvature, left contact and $q\\leq1$ is given in\n<span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"qualitative-terminal-gap\">[long §5, “A shorter qualitative exclusion”](../long/reasoning-record.md#qualitative-terminal-gap)</span>.\nThe boundary estimate retained here also describes the terminal\nbehaviour of an arbitrary coefficient constant near one.\nThe zero-coefficient heat\nargument and late-step variation have antecedents in\n[Chen, Handschy and Lerman (2018), Theorem 6 and its proof][CHL].\n\nThese cases exhaust the complement of the relatively closed set $S$.\nConsequently $S=[0,1)$, contact gives $q(t)=t$, and differentiation\ngives $\\mathbb E a^2=1$, including the right-hand value at zero.\nMoreover,\n$\\gamma(t_2)-\\gamma(t_1)=\\mu_\\gamma((t_1,t_2])>0$.\nThis proves Theorem 1 using the stated analytic inputs.\n","span":{"start_byte":11094,"end_byte":14855,"start_line":234,"end_line":312},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/short?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=short&passage=gap-proof","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=short&passage=gap-proof"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:a27286323604d97b33cbece7bb75d3d3291f7d7ec1ed0bfe12af548d92ff72dc/papers/expository.md/sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/passages/endpoints","anchor":"endpoints","mapping_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n### 5.4 The remaining initial and terminal gaps\n\n**Initial gap with zero outside the support.** Suppose $[0,s)$ is\nmissing and $s<1$ is the first support point. Then $\\gamma=0$ there,\nso the positive-coefficient bound supplies no strictness. Instead the\nzero-coefficient heat identity gives\n\n$$\nq''(t)=\\mathbb E[\\Phi_{xxx}(t,X_t)^2]>0\\qquad(0<t<s).\n$$\n\nHere is why the inequality is strict. On this interval $X_t=B_t$,\nwhose density is positive everywhere at positive times. If the expected\nsquare were zero, continuity would force $\\Phi_{xxx}(t,x)=0$ for\nevery $x$: a nonzero value would give a small interval on which the\nsquare is positive, and that interval has positive probability.\nThen $\\Phi(t,\\cdot)$ would be a quadratic polynomial, and its bounded\nslope would make it affine, a linear function plus a constant.\nBut choosing zero control gives\n$\\Phi_\\gamma(t,x)\\ge\\mathbb E|x+B_1-B_t|\\ge|x|$: the average\nabsolute value is at least the absolute value of the average. This\nrules out an affine value: domination of $|x|$ in the positive tail\nrequires slope at least one, while the negative tail requires slope\nat most minus one. The heat identity and the needed regularity are\nin [OpenAI (2026a), Lemmas 2.2–2.3][support-regularity].\n\nThus $q'$ is strictly increasing, and right contact gives\n$q'(t)<q'(s)\\le1$ for $0<t<s$. Symmetry supplies $q(0)=0$,\nwhile contact gives $q(s)=s$. Integrating again gives too little area.\nStrictness was needed only at positive times; symmetry can make the\nthird spatial derivative vanish at $(0,0)$.\n\nAlthough the forward path is Brownian here, the backward value receives\ndata $\\Phi_\\gamma(s,\\cdot)$ from the later coefficient. The formula\nfor the globally zero candidate cannot be reused on this initial\ninterval. The case where zero itself is supported was already included\nin Section 5.3, using its atom and the one-sided slope bound.\n\n```{=latex}\n\\noindent\\begin{minipage}{\\linewidth}\n```\n\n**Empty support.** This would mean $\\gamma=0$ everywhere. The zero\ncandidate fails the first-variation test:\n\n$$\nD\\mathcal P(0)[1]=\\frac14-\\frac1\\pi<0.\n$$\n\nThe direction adds an atom at zero and is admissible. Section 7.1\ncomputes the derivative from the explicit Gaussian response; no\nminimiser identity is used. A minimiser therefore has nonempty support.\n\n```{=latex}\n\\end{minipage}\n```\n\n**Terminal gap.** Now suppose a maximal missing interval is $(r,1)$.\nIts left endpoint $r<1$ is supported, and its constant height is\npositive, by the same mass argument as in Section 5.3. We have\n$q(r)=r$ and $q'(r)\\le1$, and $q'$ is strictly convex on $(r,1)$.\nThere is no contact condition at one. We use the bound $q\\le1$\ninstead. We will show that $q$ stays below the diagonal throughout\nthe gap, which makes the tail cost $G(r)$ negative. If it reached\nthe diagonal, the area comparison would force its slope above one;\nconvexity would preserve enough of that excess to violate $q\\le1$.\n\nPut $h(t)=q'(t)-1$, so $h(r)\\le0$. If $q(t)\\ge t$ at some\n$r<t<1$, strict integration below the chord on the finite interval\n$[r,t]$ would give\n\n$$\n0\\le q(t)-t=\\int_r^t h(v)\\,dv\n<\\frac{t-r}{2}\\bigl(h(r)+h(t)\\bigr)\n\\le\\frac{t-r}{2}h(t).\n$$\n\nHence $h(t)>0$. To control a later slope, use the same chord rule\non the larger interval $[r,s]$, where $t<s<1$. At its interior\npoint $t$ this gives\n\n$$\nh(t)\\le\\frac{s-t}{s-r}h(r)+\\frac{t-r}{s-r}h(s).\n$$\n\nSolving for the right endpoint gives\n\n$$\nh(s)\\ge h(t)+\\frac{s-t}{t-r}\\bigl(h(t)-h(r)\\bigr)\\ge h(t)>0.\n$$\n\nConsequently $q(s)\\ge t+(s-t)(1+h(t))$. Choose any time\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{1-t}{1+h(t)}<s<1.\n$$\n\nSuch a choice exists because $h(t)>0$, and the lower bound now gives\n$q(s)>1$, contradicting $q(s)\\le1$ strictly before time one.\nThus $q(t)<t$ throughout $(r,1)$, giving\n$G(r)=\\int_r^1(q(t)-t)\\,dt<0$, contrary to the addition test.\nOnly finite-interval convexity and the bound on $q$ were used; no\nvalue for $q'(1)$ was assumed.\n\nThe source's different terminal argument proves the stronger estimate\n$1-q(t)\\ge C\\sqrt{1-t}$ near one for any finite constant terminal\ncoefficient, without the left contact data used here. Its proof is\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3][support-variation] and is retained in the\n*Long Reasoning Record*,\n<span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"terminal-gap\">[§5, terminal estimate](../long/reasoning-record.md#terminal-gap)</span>.\n[Auffinger–Chen–Zeng, Remark 7][acz] had already recorded the absence\nof terminal plateaus.\n\nThese cases exhaust the complement of the support. Contact now gives\n$q(t)=t$ for every interior time, and symmetry includes zero.\nDifferentiating gives $\\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)^2]=1$, completing the theorem.\n","span":{"start_byte":33990,"end_byte":38668,"start_line":795,"end_line":911},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/expository?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=expository&passage=endpoints","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=expository&passage=endpoints"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=support.no_initial_gap&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=support.no_initial_gap&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=support.no_initial_gap&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=support.no_initial_gap&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:7b3670b309b6a5f48332630dbe7d3cb4036ed7155f610d71637041607339181d","from":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.no_initial_gap","to":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.paper_minimizer","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/support.no_initial_gap","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/edge